---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="Variable Speed Circulators | Smokeless Heat Blog" description="Using the Nimbus control for ideal flow rates with circulators in wood boiler and thermal storage systems.">
  <Header />

  <main class="pt-24">
    <section class="py-16 bg-charcoal text-cream">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
        <p class="text-ember text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.3em] mb-4">Installation</p>
        <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-5xl">Variable Speed Circulators</h1>
      </div>
    </section>

    <article class="py-16 bg-cream">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 space-y-8 text-charcoal/90 leading-relaxed">

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-start">
          <div>
            <p>The Nimbus control lets you achieve the ideal flow rate for any circulator in your system. Flow rate matters. A boiler circulator that runs too slowly can overheat, while one that runs too quickly can reduce stratification in thermal storage tanks by mixing the hot and cold water.</p>
            <p class="mt-4">With the manual dip switch settings on the Nimbus, you can test different speeds to find the best flow rate for transferring heat.</p>
            <p class="mt-4 italic">The default settings have all switches in the off position, as shown here.</p>
            <img src="https://smokelessheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2010/01/DefaultSettings.jpg" alt="Default dip switch settings" class="mt-4 border border-wood/20" />
            <p class="text-sm text-center mt-1">Default dip switch settings</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <img src="https://smokelessheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/03/FixedSpeedSettings.jpg" alt="Table 7: Fixed Speed Settings" class="border border-wood/20" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>Using the dip switches, you can manually set the controller to a fixed speed. It can be adjusted in 1% increments from 27% to 100% output. Note that most circulators will not operate reliably below 40% when controlled by the Nimbus.</p>

        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-8 justify-center items-start">
          <div class="text-center">
            <img src="https://smokelessheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2010/01/FixedSpeed27.jpg" alt="Position for fixed speed control (27%)" class="border border-wood/20 max-w-full" />
            <p class="text-sm mt-2">Position for fixed speed control (27%)</p>
          </div>
          <div class="text-center">
            <img src="https://smokelessheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2010/01/FixedSpeed50.jpg" alt="Example of fixed speed at 50%" class="border border-wood/20 max-w-full" />
            <p class="text-sm mt-2">Example of fixed speed at 50%</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>Used this way, the Nimbus controller gives you a single user-defined speed for almost any circulator. Our experience has been primarily with Taco circulators (007, 0010, and 0014 models).</p>

        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-darkwood mt-12">Using Nimbus for True Variable Speed with NFCS</h2>

        <p>Another option is to use the Nimbus with a NoFossil Control System (now called VESTA), also known as NFCS. The analog outputs (AO) in the left-hand column are the four outputs controlled by a 4-20 mA signal.</p>

        <img src="https://smokelessheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2010/01/LiveShot.jpg" alt="NoFossil Control System Web Interface" class="mx-auto border border-wood/20" />

        <p>The advantage of pairing the Nimbus with the NFCS is that it can adjust circulator speeds dynamically based on current heating system temperatures and rule sets. This provides greater control and helps the system run at its most efficient settings.</p>

        <p>In this configuration, the NFCS controls the Nimbus through a 4-20 mA signal. The NFCS connects to each Nimbus controller with a signal wire that runs through a variable-speed breakout box.</p>

        <p>One of the first steps is to set the jumper so the controller accepts a 4-20 mA signal.</p>

        <div class="text-center">
          <img src="https://smokelessheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2010/01/JumperSettings.jpg" alt="Jumper is shown in correct position for 4-20ma as used with NFCS" class="mx-auto border border-wood/20" />
          <p class="text-sm mt-2">Jumper is shown in correct position for 4-20 mA as used with NFCS.</p>
        </div>

        <p>The manual helps you choose the best settings for your system. The example below is configured for current control and is set to run at 60% if no signal is present. Where the manual refers to a “fan,” it applies to the circulator in this application.</p>

        <p><strong>A. Fan On / Fan Off Feature (switch #1):</strong> To turn the fan(s) off below the set idle speed (see Table 3), set switch #1 to the ON position. To keep the fans running at idle speed below the set idle speed, set switch #1 to the OFF position.</p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
          <img src="https://smokelessheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2010/01/table3.jpg" alt="Table 3: Idle Speed DIP Switch Settings" class="border border-wood/20" />
          <img src="https://smokelessheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2010/01/dipswitch.jpg" alt="Dip switch diagram" class="border border-wood/20" />
        </div>

        <p><strong>C. Control Signal Loss Options (switch #6):</strong> If the control signal is lost (less than 4 mA in I mode, or less than 2 VDC in V mode) and switch 6 is OFF, the fans will continue to idle or remain off. To send the fans to full speed if the control signal is lost, set switch 6 to the ON position.</p>

        <img src="https://smokelessheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2010/01/table2.jpg" alt="Table 2: Setting Control Modes" class="mx-auto border border-wood/20" />

        <p>If the NFCS is offline and not supplying 4-20 mA signals, the Nimbus can be set to run at one of four preselected idle speeds or to run wide open, provided the aquastat or other control switch is supplying line voltage to the Nimbus.</p>

        <img src="https://smokelessheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2010/01/FallBackNimbus.jpg" alt="Fallback Nimbus wiring diagram" class="mx-auto border border-wood/20" />

        <p class="mt-8">Connecting a variable speed control unit to the NFCS</p>

        <div class="mt-12">
          <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-darkwood mb-4">Video</h2>
          <div class="aspect-video w-full">
            <iframe 
              width="100%" 
              height="100%" 
              src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/4JPbp7EnWRQ" 
              title="Variable Speed Circulators Video" 
              frameborder="0" 
              allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" 
              allowfullscreen
              class="rounded border border-wood/20"
            ></iframe>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
